abstract: >
  AutoApply (AA) is an open-source job application platform that doubles as a
  research instrument for studying hiring-system dysfunction. Its central claim
  is that a local program on commodity hardware, with no network AI, no API keys
  and no site-specific selectors, can comprehend an unfamiliar web interface well
  enough to operate it and produce a verifiable record of what it encountered.
  Page understanding is geometric rather than selector-driven: Kuhn-Munkres
  assignment pairs labels with inputs, convex hulls and VIPS-style segmentation
  identify regions, and structural hashing deliberately ignores CSS class names.
  AA implements a hexagonal (ports and adapters) architecture with seeded,
  reproducible execution, and exports consent-gated, anonymised observations as
  NDJSON, CSV and JSON-LD for archival deposit. AA is pre-alpha software; at the
  date of this record it has not yet completed an end-to-end application
  submission, and that result is published rather than withheld.
